    deeperDive: [
      { q: 'How does this build on the previous critique?', a: 'V.4 negated independence (reductio). V.5 positively articulates the alternative: mutual dependence. Nāgārjuna moves from showing what\'s FALSE (independence) to showing what\'s TRUE (mutual disclosure). This is the constructive moment of the argument.', realLifeExample: 'After proving you can\'t drive on flat tires (negative), you explain that properly inflated tires are needed (positive). Destruction then construction.' },
      { q: 'What does "disclosed" (pratijñāyate) mean?', a: 'Making known, revealing, establishing, acknowledging. Subject and object MAKE each other known. The self doesn\'t exist and then get to know objects; the very knowing of objects IS what constitutes the self. And the objects are constituted by being known by a subject.', realLifeExample: 'An artist is "disclosed" (revealed as an artist) by their art. The art is "disclosed" (revealed as art) by the artist. Neither exists as such without the other.' },
      { q: 'Does mutual dependence mean subject = object?', a: 'No. Subject and object remain conventionally DISTINCT. What\'s denied is their INDEPENDENT existence, not their distinction. They are like two aspects of one process — different yet inseparable.', realLifeExample: 'Buying and selling are different activities but inseparable — you can\'t have one without the other. The buyer is not the seller, but neither exists without the transaction.' },
      { q: 'What is Relational Quantum Mechanics?', a: 'Proposed by Carlo Rovelli in 1996, RQM holds that ALL quantum properties (spin, position, momentum) exist only relative to the system observing them. There is no absolute, observer-independent quantum state. Properties are relations, not intrinsic attributes.', realLifeExample: 'Speed only exists relative to a reference frame — there\'s no "absolute speed." RQM extends this relativity to ALL quantum properties: there\'s no "absolute quantum state."' },
      { q: 'How does this avoid both dualism and monism?', a: 'Dualism: subject and object are completely separate (refuted in v.4). Monism: subject and object are identical (refuted — they\'re conventionally distinct). Mutual dependence is the middle way: distinct yet inseparable, different yet co-arising.', realLifeExample: 'Music and listening: music isn\'t the same as listening (not monism), but music without a listener and a listener without music are both incomplete (not dualism). They\'re distinct but co-arising.' },
      { q: 'Why is this verse the philosophical heart of the chapter?', a: 'It articulates the positive thesis: mutual dependence of subject and object. This is not just a critique but a VISION of how reality works — subject and object co-arise, mutually constitute, and are empty of independent existence. This IS pratītyasamutpāda.', deeper: 'This verse connects to MMK 24:18 (dependent origination = emptiness) and to the entire Mādhyamika project. It\'s also remarkably close to RQM\'s relational ontology — one of the strongest philosophy-physics parallels in the MMK.' },
    ],
