    deeperDive: [
      { q: 'What position is Nāgārjuna targeting?', a: 'The Sarvāstivāda Abhidharma position that reality consists of momentary dharmas (phenomena) with inherent nature (svabhāva). Even if these dharmas are momentary rather than permanent, they possess inherent existence at each moment. Nāgārjuna refutes this too.', realLifeExample: 'Switching from a permanent contract to a series of one-day contracts doesn\'t solve the underlying problem if each contract is still problematic.' },
      { q: 'Why doesn\'t momentariness solve the problem?', a: 'Because the problem isn\'t DURATION — it\'s INHERENT EXISTENCE. Whether a self supposedly exists for an eon or a microsecond, if it\'s inherently existent, it faces all the same problems (epistemic inaccessibility, independence paradox, etc.). Shrinking the time scale doesn\'t eliminate inherent existence.', realLifeExample: 'A very small square circle is still a square circle. Making it smaller doesn\'t resolve the logical contradiction.' },
      { q: 'What is the Quantum Zeno Effect?', a: 'If you measure a quantum system continuously, its state is "frozen" — it can\'t evolve. Frequent measurement prevents change. This shows that trying to pin down states at ever-finer intervals creates paradoxes rather than solving them.', realLifeExample: 'Watching a pot "never boils" (folk version). Quantum version: if you check a radioactive atom constantly, it literally won\'t decay.' },
      { q: 'How does this close a loophole in the argument?', a: 'Without this verse, the opponent could concede "no permanent self" but retreat to "momentary selves." V.7 blocks this retreat: the same arguments against a permanent prior self apply equally to momentary prior selves. Emptiness holds at EVERY temporal scale.', realLifeExample: 'Plugging a leak in an argument: "You can\'t escape the conclusion by making the self smaller. The logic applies at every scale."' },
      { q: 'What does this mean for Buddhist practice?', a: 'Even moment-to-moment self-grasping is unfounded. Whether you grasp at a permanent self or a momentary self, the grasping is based on a false view of inherent existence. Letting go of BOTH is part of the path to liberation.', realLifeExample: 'Clinging to a permanent job or to a series of gig jobs — both involve clinging. The form changes; the grasping remains.' },
      { q: 'How does Nāgārjuna differ from the Abhidharma here?', a: 'Abhidharma accepted the Buddha\'s teaching of impermanence (no permanent self) but preserved inherent existence for momentary dharmas. Nāgārjuna goes further: EVEN momentary dharmas lack inherent existence. Emptiness is more radical than impermanence.', deeper: 'This verse is key to understanding the difference between Abhidharma and Mādhyamika: impermanence (accepting momentary inherent existence) vs. emptiness (denying inherent existence at ANY scale). Nāgārjuna\'s innovation is the comprehensive application of emptiness.' },
    ],
